---

<BaseLayout
  title="Chilli Heat Explained: SHU vs Capsaicin | Tenda Peppers"
  description="How chilli heat works — SHU (Scoville Heat Units), capsaicin, and why heat is non-linear. A guide for chilli and paprika buyers. Tenda Peppers Knowledge Hub."
  ogType="article"
  author="Tenda Peppers Technical Team"
  datePublished="2026-09-01"
  dateModified="2026-09-01"
  jsonLd={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#6b5a54;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/blog/">Guides</a> &rsaquo; Chilli Heat Explained
    </nav>
    <h1>Chilli Heat Explained: SHU vs Capsaicin</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Chilli heat is measured in Scoville Heat Units (SHU) — a proxy for capsaicin concentration. But
      heat is not flavour, and it is not linear. Understanding both is what makes a specification
      reliable.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What SHU actually measures</h2>
    <p>
      SHU quantifies the concentration of capsaicin, the compound that triggers the heat sensation on
      the palate. Sweet pepper registers under 500 SHU; the hottest common Chinese varieties — Jixin,
      Chilli King — exceed 50,000 SHU. The number is a concentration, not a "taste strength", which is
      why two chillies can share an SHU rating yet feel very different.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Heat is non-linear — dose accordingly</h2>
    <p>
      A small amount of a 50,000+ SHU variety moves a blend's heat far more than the same weight of a
      10,000 SHU variety. In practice this means extra-hot chillies are usually blended as an accent,
      and precise SHU windows — not "hot" or "medium" — are what keep a product consistent batch to
      batch.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Heat vs colour vs flavour</h2>
    <p>
      Three separate properties, controlled independently. Heat (capsaicin, SHU) is what burns; colour
      (carotenoids, ASTA) is what tints red; flavour (aroma compounds) is what you taste. A complete
      specification names all three — which is why a variety name beats a generic "chilli powder".
      See <a href="/heat-levels/high-heat/">high heat</a> and
      <a href="/specs/mesh-size/">mesh size</a> for the practical next steps.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is SHU and how is chilli heat measured?</h3>
      <p>SHU (Scoville Heat Units) measures chilli heat as the concentration of capsaicin — the compound that triggers the heat sensation. The higher the SHU, the hotter the chilli: sweet pepper is under 500 SHU, while extra-hot varieties exceed 50,000 SHU.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Is chilli heat linear?</h3>
      <p>No. Heat is non-linear: a small amount of a 50,000+ SHU variety moves a blend's heat far more than the same weight of a 10,000 SHU variety. That is why precise SHU windows matter in a specification.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is the difference between heat and flavour?</h3>
      <p>Heat (capsaicin, measured in SHU) and flavour (aroma compounds) are separate and vary independently. Two chillies can share the same SHU but taste completely different — another reason to name the variety, not just the heat.</p>
    </div>

